    "text": "The ash tree belongs to the genus Fraxinus. It grows in many climates. It plays a role in Scandinavian, Germanic, and Celtic mythology. It is one of the magical trees of Celtic tradition. The oak and the thorn join it in that group. People linked the ash with fairies. They also believed it could ward fairies off. Scots Highland mothers burned a green ash branch. They fed the oozing sap to a newborn as first food. People used ash whip handles to ward off witchcraft. People passed children through split ash branches to cure rickets. Ashwood was a general charm against evil. The ash is tied to Beltane, the May 1 festival. In Ireland the chief Beltane site was the hill of Uisneach. Ash trees there were especially potent. One great ash on Uisneach once fell in prehistory. Its tip reached near Longford. Its height suggested a world-axis. Another famous ash was the holy tree of Clenore. The spirit Creeva lived there. Three of the five great mythological trees were ash. The rowan is a different genus from the ash.",
